From: Zurker <91011862+hershate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 04:15:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Papo: batch 149 (R9) canonical shared constant chunk
 sections. The section/container instance layer was the largest remaining
 non-content cluster: 398,976 LevelChunkSection shells (40B) + 797,953
 PalettedContainer shells (24B, two per section) + 398,976 ShortList shells
 (24B, lazily mapped since batch 142) = 112B per section, and 87% of sections
 are constant (uniform states + uniform biome: everything above the heightmap
 plus uniform unexplored underground). A constant section now collapses onto
 ONE canonical instance per (blockState global id, biome registry id) per
 level registry - all four shells drop to zero per chunk. UNIFORMITY IS ABOUT
 THE CELLS, NOT THE PALETTE TYPE: the first paired soak with a
 SingleValuePalette-only predicate collapsed just 1.4% of sections because
 worldgen builds uniform sections through the write path with GROWN palettes
 (a biome container whose resolved biome differs from the default resizes to a
 one-entry linear palette on its first quart write; a written all-stone
 section likewise) - PalettedContainer.papoUniformValue() now decides by cell
 content: zero-bit storage fast path (every cell is id 0), then raw-word
 equality as a fast reject, then a per-cell pass to settle equal-word cases.
 Design: the canonical cache hangs on the per-registry biome Strategy instance
 so it dies with the level and biome ids cannot collide across levels (batch
 147 judgment #3; block-state ids are global); canonical containers are built
 through the batch 147 single-value Data sharing hooks, so they also share
 Data with the world's other constant containers; canonical counters are
 analytic (nonEmpty = isAir ? 0 : 4096, tick/special counters zero) and the
 collapse predicate defensively verifies the section's own counters match
 before sharing. Writer audit closed the mutation surface to six gates:
 LevelChunk.setBlockState and ProtoChunk.setBlockState (both behind the
 existing air-into-air no-op fast path, so the most common write never
 materialises), ChunkAccess.setBiome, ChunkAccess.fillBiomesFromNoise,
 NoiseBasedChunkGenerator doFill's two acquisition points, and OreFeature
 through a new BulkSectionAccess.getWritableSection (read-path getSection
 stays untouched; the writable variant replaces any cached canonical entry,
 transferring the acquire). Client packet paths
 (replaceWithPacketData/replaceBiomes) gate via indexed loops. Every
 LevelChunkSection mutator
 (setBlockState/setBiome/fillBiomesFromNoise/read/readBiomes/recalcBlockCounts)
 carries a loud canary that throws on canonical mutation - an ungated writer
 fails immediately instead of corrupting every chunk sharing the instance. The
 legacy Bukkit generator path (CustomChunkGenerator) injects private scratch
 section objects by whole-slot replacement, which is safe without a gate.
 Canonicalisation happens at the single LevelChunk main-ctor scan (both
 creation paths - worldgen promotion and SerializableChunkData disk load -
 arrive there; blank-slot replacement sections too), using the level's
 long-lived factory so cache hits are per-world. Randomly-ticking /
 fluid-ticking / special-colliding uniforms, mixed sections, client sections
 and unregistered biome holders stay private by predicate; a canonical section
 saves as a single-value palette, which decodes identically on every version
 (vanilla's own encoding for never-grown sections) and is smaller. Expected
 effect: 347k uniform sections x 112B = ~38MB (4.8% of the 801MB soak heap).
 Coverage: PapoSharedConstantSectionTest 11 tests (collapse identity +
 per-registry cache isolation, analytic counts + per-biome keys, per-strategy
 instance isolation, uniform-WRITTEN sections collapse - the regression the
 first soak caught, ticking/mixed/unregistered rejections, copy-for-write
 exact values + write isolation, canaries on all six mutators, writable-gate
 slot replacement + pass-through identity, and a 300-round x 120-write
 randomized differential of gated writes against always-private mirrors using
 real block states via Bootstrap and a synthetic biome IdMap).


